y in an R+8 state, boosted by two late Trump rallies and roughly $2.6M in outside support", "Andrews has out-raised Graham about $24M to $1.9M, keeping the race competitive despite minimal outside help", "Prediction markets price Graham near 80% and the expert ratings consensus is Lean R"], "rationale": "Polling shows a single-digit contest, but Graham's incumbency, South Carolina's Republican lean, Trump's late-campaign rallies, and an outside-spending edge outweigh Andrews' large fundraising advantage, pointing to a modest Republican lead.", "watch": "Fresh surveys from higher-quality pollsters, or a sharp late shift in the national environment, could push the race toward a genuine tossup or solidify Graham's edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pairs appointed Republican Senator Darline Graham against Democrat Annie Andrews, a first-time candidate who has raised about $24 million to Graham's $1.9 million. Recent polls show a surprisingly tight contest, with most surveys putting Graham ahead by one to four points and one Democratic-sponsored poll showing the race essentially tied. The state's Republican lean, Graham's incumbency, and outside groups spending roughly $2.6 million to back her give the Republican structural advantages that Andrews' individual fundraising has not fully offset.\n\nPresident Trump has campaigned for Graham twice in the final weeks, and prediction markets give her roughly an 80 percent chance of winning. Expert race ratings mostly call the contest Lean Republican. Graham holds a clear but not commanding advantage; the polling tightness and Andrews' financial edge leave room for an upset if the national environment breaks hard against Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-